import Native

{-
   The Castagnoli checksum over the `crc32` instruction - SSE4.2, and therefore x86-64-v2, and
   therefore this backend's floor.

   There is no feature test and no fallback path here, and that is the point of the floor:
   `kFeatureBaseline` *is* v2 (see `codegen/x64/target.h`), so a native build of this compiler
   already requires SSE4.2 and a `crc32` form is always selectable. The table in `Crc.yana` is the
   JS implementation and the reference this is checked against, not a tier below this one.

   Eight bytes at a time through the 64-bit form and the remainder a byte at a time. The 64-bit
   `crc32` reads a `U64` and answers a value in the low thirty-two bits of a 64-bit register, which
   is why `X86.crc32` takes and answers what it does - see `lib/Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana`.

   **The dependency chain is one instruction long per eight bytes**, which is what separates this
   from the table: `crc32` on Intel has a latency of three cycles and a throughput of one per cycle,
   so a single chained stream runs at eight bytes per three cycles - about 8 GB/s at 3 GHz - and the
   three-way interleaved form that reaches the throughput limit needs the polynomial-multiply
   constants to recombine the streams. That is a `pclmulqdq` scheme, which is outside this backend's
   feature set; the single stream is what is here and it is already an order of magnitude past the
   table.

   **The word read is `Core`'s `readLittleU64` and not a raw load written here**, bounds check
   included. That check is five instructions the loop would not otherwise have and it is measured at
   nothing - 24.0 ms against 23.9 ms over 200 MiB, which is inside the run-to-run spread - because
   the loop is bound by the three-cycle `crc32` chain and the compare has an idle port to run on.
   A latency-bound loop is exactly where a safe read is free, and a hand-written `*(bitcast(...))`
   would be a second copy of `Core/Container.yana`'s reader with the check taken out of it.
-}
pub fn crc32cUpdate(value: U32, from: [U8], at: Size, count: Size) -> U32:
    let &crc = value
    let &i = at
    let stop = at + count
    let bulk = stop - 8

    while i <= bulk:
        crc = crcWide(crc, readLittleU64(from, i))
        i = i + 8

    while i < stop:
        crc = crcByte(crc, from[i])
        i = i + 1

    return crc

{-
   The two folds the loop above is written in terms of - the instruction, and the table it falls back
   to for a tail.

   The remainder is thirty-two bits and the chunk is sixty-four, so `crcWide` widens and truncates
   around the call - and neither conversion survives: the 64-bit `crc32` reads its remainder from the
   low half of the destination and writes a zero-extended answer, so what the two conversions
   describe is what the instruction already does.

   **The tail is the table and not a narrower instruction.** The machine has `crc32 r32, r/m8`; the
   lower IR has no scalar below `Int32`, so nothing above the backend can say that a chunk is one
   byte wide, and a form that could not be told apart from the 32-bit one is a form nobody can
   select. What that costs is at most seven table lookups per message and nothing per block - see
   `lib/Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana`, where the width set is argued.
-}
fn crcWide(value: U32, word: U64) -> U32 = truncate(X86.crc32(widen(value) :: U64, word)) :: U32

fn crcByte(value: U32, byte: U8) -> U32 =
    (value `shr` 8) `xor` crcCastagnoliTable[truncate((value `xor` (byte :: U32)) `and` 255) :: Size]
